    {
      "dom_id": "question-130028",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution.",
      "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)t.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}t+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′})+2t({u}^{′}t+u)-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+4w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=ut$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2})t.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
