    {
      "dom_id": "question-343061",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+3L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-1)+3Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s)-1 & =\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+3)Y(s) & =1+\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+4}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+s+6}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+s+6}{(s+3)({s}^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}+s+6}{(s+3)({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}+s+6=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+3)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(-3)^{2}+(-3)+6=A((-3)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,12=13A\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n1=A+B\\,⟹\\,1=\\frac{12}{13}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n6=4A+3C\\,⟹\\,6=4(\\frac{12}{13})+3C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{10}{13}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{(\\frac{12}{13})}{13(s+3)}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{13}s+\\frac{10}{13})}{{s}^{2}+4}=\\frac{12}{13(s+3)}+\\frac{s+10}{13({s}^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} & ={e}^{-3t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{12}{13}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}}+\\frac{1}{13}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+\\frac{5}{13}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{12}{13}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\frac{1}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
