ath/MathML\"><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>sin</mi><mo data-mjx-texclass=\"NONE\">⁡</mo><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"INNER\"><mo data-mjx-texclass=\"OPEN\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo data-mjx-texclass=\"CLOSE\">)</mo></mrow></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></div></div></div>, where <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"2099\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-msub><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D435 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: -0.15em;\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45B TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-script></mjx-msub><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2208\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-texatom space=\"4\" texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-ds mjx-b\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c211D TEX-A\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-texatom><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyEasy\">E</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Sun, Oct 11th @ 3:37 AM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 4:52</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Correct</span></span></div></div>"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "question-345296",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+3{y}^{′}(t)+2y(t)={u}_{2}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+3sY(s)+2Y(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-{e}^{-(t-2)}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+3sY(s)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+3s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s} \\\\ (s+1)(s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s(s+1)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s(s+1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{s+2}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=\\frac{1}{2}$, $B=-1$, and $C=\\frac{1}{2}$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+3s+2)}=\\frac{1}{2s}-\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{2(s+2)}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{2s}-\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{2(s+2)},\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\frac{1}{2}-{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{e}^{-2s}F(s)} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{2}(t)\\,f(t-2) \\\\ y(t) & =u(t-2)(\\frac{1}{2}-{e}^{-(t-2)}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2(t-2)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
