    {
      "dom_id": "question-95307",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Incorrect",
